{"id": "668", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is what happens when you breathe in and out:\n\nWhen you breathe in (inhalation):\n\n* The intercostal muscles contract, causing the ribcage to expand.\n* The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest.\n* Pressure inside the chest is lowered, allowing air to be sucked into the lungs.\n\nWhen you breathe out (exhalation):\n\n* The diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity.\n* The intercostal muscles relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity.\n* Air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.\n\nSo, in summary, when you breathe in, the diaphragm and intercostal muscles contract to bring air into the lungs, and when you breathe out, the diaphragm and intercostal muscles relax to force air out of the lungs.", "pred": "Conversely, when you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, increasing the pressure inside your chest cavity. The intercostal muscles also relax, causing your ribcage to contract inward. This decreases the volume of your chest cavity, which increases the air pressure inside your lungs. As a result, air is expelled from your lungs to equalize the pressure difference.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "When you breathe in, air moves into your lungs. This occurs when the diaphragm, a large, dome-shaped muscle at the base of the lungs, contracts and moves downward. As the diaphragm contracts, it reduces the pressure inside your chest cavity. Simultaneously, the intercostal muscles between your ribs contract, causing your ribcage to expand outward. This combined action increases the volume of your chest cavity, which lowers the air pressure inside. As a result, air rushes into your lungs to equalize the pressure difference. \n\nConversely, when you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, increasing the pressure inside your chest cavity. The intercostal muscles also relax, causing your ribcage to contract inward. This decreases the volume of your chest cavity, which increases the air pressure inside your lungs. As a result, air is expelled from your lungs to equalize the pressure difference.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nReview the given passages for specific details about the physiological process of breathing, including the movement of air in and out of the lungs.\nAnalyze the physiological mechanisms involved in inhalation and exhalation, focusing on the diaphragm, intercostal muscles, and the role of air movement within the respiratory system.\nExplain the movement of air in the respiratory system during inhalation and exhalation, highlighting the role of the diaphragm and intercostal muscles.\n\nWhen you breathe in, air moves into your lungs. This occurs when the diaphragm, a large, dome-shaped muscle at the base of the lungs, contracts and moves downward. As the diaphragm contracts, it reduces the pressure inside your chest cavity. Simultaneously, the intercostal muscles between your ribs contract, causing your ribcage to expand outward. This combined action increases the volume of your chest cavity, which lowers the air pressure inside. As a result, air rushes into your lungs to equalize the pressure difference. \n\nConversely, when you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, increasing the pressure inside your chest cavity. The intercostal muscles also relax, causing your ribcage to contract inward. This decreases the volume of your chest cavity, which increases the air pressure inside your lungs. As a result, air is expelled from your lungs to equalize the pressure difference.", "time_sec": 32.996, "generation_time_seconds": 30.3642, "selection_time_seconds": 2.6243, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [785, 3405, 