{"id": "428", "question": "how do twins happen", "gt": "Twins are two offspring produced by the same pregnancy. They can be either monozygotic (identical), meaning they develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), meaning they develop from two different eggs with each being fertilized by its own sperm cell. Monoamniotic twins occur when the split takes place after the ninth day after fertilization. Conjoined twins occur when the division of the developing zygote into 2 embryos occurs within 8 days of fertilization. Dizygotic twin pregnancies are more likely in women of West African descent, especially Yoruba, aged between 30 and 40 years, of above average height and weight, and who have had several previous pregnancies.", "pred": "Environmental factors such as exposure to certain chemicals or medications can also influence the likelihood of having twins. Additionally, assisted reproductive technologies such as in vitro fertilization (IVF) can increase the chances of having twins, as multiple embryos are often transferred during IVF to increase the likelihood of pregnancy. These embryos can be either fraternal or identical twins, depending on the genetic makeup of the eggs and sperm used in the procedure.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "Twins can occur through two main mechanisms: fraternal (dizygotic) twins and identical (monozygotic) twins.\n\nFraternal twins are the most common type of twins and occur when two eggs are fertilized by two different sperm cells, resulting in two separate embryos. This process is random and not influenced by specific genetic factors. Fraternal twins can be of any sex, and the occurrence of fraternal twins is not typically associated with the mother’s age or health. However, fraternal twins can occur more frequently if the mother has a history of fraternal twins or if she is taking fertility drugs that increase the chances of ovulating multiple eggs.\n\nIdentical twins, on the other hand, occur when a single fertilized egg splits into two embryos early in development. This split can occur very early, resulting in identical twins that share the same genetic makeup and physical characteristics. Identical twins can occur in both sexes and are not influenced by factors such as the mother’s age or health. However, the likelihood of identical twins occurring increases with the mother’s age, as the chances of a single fertilized egg splitting increase with age.\n\nEnvironmental factors such as exposure to certain chemicals or medications can also influence the likelihood of having twins. Additionally, assisted reproductive technologies such as in vitro fertilization (IVF) can increase the chances of having twins, as multiple embryos are often transferred during IVF to increase the likelihood of pregnancy. These embryos can be either fraternal or identical twins, depending on the genetic makeup of the eggs and sperm used in the procedure.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nThe reasoning steps are:\n1. Identify the types of twins that can occur.\n2. Explain the genetic and biological processes involved in each type of twin formation.\n3. Discuss the role of factors such as age, health, and lifestyle in increasing the chances of having twins.\n4. Explain how environmental factors and assisted reproductive technologies can influence the likelihood of having twins.\n\nTwins can occur through two main mechanisms: fraternal (dizygotic) twins and identical (monozygotic) twins.\n\nFraternal twins are the most common type of twins and occur when two eggs are fertilized by two different sperm cells, resulting in two separate embryos. This process is random and not influenced by specific genetic factors. Fraternal twins can be of any sex, and the occurrence of fraternal twins is not typically associated with the mother’s age or health. However, fraternal twins can occur more frequently if the mother has a history of fraternal twins or if she is taking fertility drugs that increase the chances of ovulating multiple eggs.\n\nIdentical twins, on the other hand, occur when a single fertilized egg splits into two embryos early in development. This split can occur very early, resulting in identical twins that share the same genetic makeup and physical characteristics. Identical twins can occur in both sexes and are not influenced by factors such as the mother’s age or health. However, the likelihood of identical twins occurring increases with the mother’s age, as the chances of a single fertilized egg splitting increase with age.\n\nEnvironmental factors such as exposure to certain chemicals or medications can also influence the likelihood of having twins. Additionally, assisted reproductive technologies such as in vitro fertilization (IVF) can increase the chances of having twins, as multiple embryos are often transferred during IVF to increase the likelihood of pregnancy. These embryos can be either fraternal or identical twins, depending on the genetic makeup of the eggs and sperm used in the procedure.", "time_sec": 40.576, "generation_time_seconds": 37.9532, "selection_time_seconds": 2.6093, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [840, 20772, 279, 23275, 1882, 6398, 304, 279, 18003, 315, 43636, 13, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645], "logp_list": [-2.3958, -0.8028, -0.1341, -0.4838, -0.1306, -1.6882, -0.0011, -0.0959, -0.1368, -0.0006, -0.0027, -0.5541, -0.0007, -30.625, -22.3958, -Infinity, -10.8334, -12.9517, -19.5833, -12.1773, -10.8619, -10.0009, -9.965, -8.0117, -9.9708, -9.8826, -9.9357, -8.3776, -7.6503, -9.1919, -8.5563, -7.1153], "cumulative_logprob": -Infinity, "norm_logp": -Infinity, "input_len": 137, "output_tokens": 32, "avg_logp": -Infinity}, {"beam": 0, "rol