{"id": "429", "question": "how do twins happen", "gt": "Twins can happen in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins occur when one zygote splits into two embryos, while dizygotic (fraternal) twins happen when two different eggs are fertilized by two separate sperm cells. The likelihood of dizygotic twins is influenced by factors such as West African descent, being between 30 and 40 years old, being taller and heavier than average, and having had several previous pregnancies. Conjoined twins occur when the division of the zygote happens within 8 days of fertilization and can result in complications due to shared organs.", "pred": "[Twins can occur through different fertilization processes. Monozygotic (identical) twins are produced from a single fertilized egg that splits into two embryos. Fraternal twins are produced from two separate eggs, each fertilized by a different sperm. Twins can be either identical or fraternal, depending on the fertilization process. Identical twins share the same genetic makeup, while fraternal twins have the same likelihood of shared genes as any siblings.]", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "Answer:[Twins can occur through different fertilization processes. Monozygotic (identical) twins are produced from a single fertilized egg that splits into two embryos. Fraternal twins are produced from two separate eggs, each fertilized by a different sperm. Twins can be either identical or fraternal, depending on the fertilization process. Identical twins share the same genetic makeup, while fraternal twins have the same likelihood of shared genes as any siblings.]", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nStep 1: Understand the basic concept of twins.\nTwins are born simultaneously, one to each parent. They can be identical twins or fraternal twins.\nStep 2: Identify the types of twins and their formation.\n- Identical twins: These twins are produced from a single fertilized egg that splits into two embryos. They share the same genetic makeup.\n- Fraternal twins: These twins are produced from two separate eggs, each fertilized by a different sperm. They have the same likelihood of shared genes as any siblings.\nStep 3: Explain the process of fertilization and embryo development.\nTwins can occur through different fertilization processes:\n- Monozygotic (identical) twins: One egg is fertilized by one sperm and then splits into two embryos.\n- Dizygotic (fraternal) twins: Two separate eggs are fertilized by two different sperm.\n\nAnswer:[Twins can occur through different fertilization processes. Monozygotic (identical) twins are produced from a single fertilized egg that splits into two embryos. Fraternal twins are produced from two separate eggs, each fertilized by a different sperm. Twins can be either identical or fraternal, depending on the fertilization process. Identical twins share the same genetic makeup, while fraternal twins have the same likelihood of shared genes as any siblings.]", "time_sec": 27.351, "generation_time_seconds": 24.6383, "selection_time_seconds": 2.7025, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [8304, 220, 16, 25, 70894, 279, 6993, 315, 43636, 624, 22816, 1330, 525, 9223, 518, 279, 1852, 882, 311, 279, 1852, 6554, 13, 1096, 3363, 429, 279, 43636, 4332, 279, 1852, 28860, 16576, 323, 36681, 321, 938, 22324, 2337, 19636, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-2.7083, -0.0018, -0.0062, -0.0004, -1.4211, -0.0274, -2.2834, -0.0013, -0.0099, -0.6376, -0.052, 0.0, -0.2825, -0.9517, -0.7808, -0.0003, 0.0, -0.0006, -0.956, -0.0173, -0.0, -0.004, -0.3173, -4.003, -1.3224, -0.5289, -1.311, -0.2351, -0.1872, -0.0676, -0.0018, -2.9422, -0.0001, -0.0141, -0.6473, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0002, -2.6245, -0.151, -1.5195, -0.2101], "cumulative_logprob": -26.2259, "norm_logp": -0.6244, "input_len": 137, "output_tokens": 42, "avg_logp": -0.6244246363639832}, {"beam": 0, "rollout": 1, "token_