{"id": "492", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, there are several ways to track who likes your tweets:\n\n1. Favotter.net and Favstar.fm - These websites allow you to track who has favorited your tweets and which tweets have been favorited by multiple users. (Passage 1)\n2. Using a web-based website - There are websites that allow you to check Twitter users who favorite tweets. (Passage 2)\n3. On Twitter - You can set up your account settings to receive notifications via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite. (Passage 3)\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question is: You can use Favotter.net, Favstar.fm, or a web-based website to track who likes your tweets, or you can set up your Twitter account settings to receive email notifications.", "pred": "To tell who liked your tweet, you should look for the list of usernames next to the like count on the social media platform. The platform should display all users who have liked the tweet. Some platforms may also provide additional options or filters to refine the list.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "Answer: To tell who liked your tweet, you should look for the list of usernames next to the like count on the social media platform. The platform should display all users who have liked the tweet. Some platforms may also provide additional options or filters to refine the list.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nThe question asks how to determine who liked a tweet. To analyze this logically, we should consider the typical process of social media interactions and how liking a tweet is recorded and displayed.\nThe typical process of liking a tweet involves users interacting with the platform through their mobile devices or web browsers. When a user likes a tweet, the action is recorded by the social media platform, which then updates the tweet's like count and displays the usernames of individuals who have liked the tweet. This information is publicly accessible on the platform, allowing others to see who has interacted with the post.\nTo determine how to tell who liked a tweet, we should focus on the actions and features of the social media platform that record and display likes. The platform should have a feature that allows users to see who liked their tweet, typically by displaying a list of usernames next to the like count. The platform may also provide additional options or filters to refine the list, such as sorting by most recent likes or by the user's followers.\n\nAnswer: To tell who liked your tweet, you should look for the list of usernames next to the like count on the social media platform. The platform should display all users who have liked the tweet. Some platforms may also provide additional options or filters to refine the list.", "time_sec": 28.058, "generation_time_seconds": 25.1064, "selection_time_seconds": 2.933, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [785, 3405, 17064, 1246, 311, 8253, 879, 14915, 264, 11809, 13, 2014, 23643, 419, 73045, 11, 582, 1265, 2908, 279, 14260, 1882, 315, 3590, 3687, 21880, 323, 1246, 48737, 264, 11809, 374, 12433, 323, 12596, 382, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.0826, -0.8, -0.0348, -0.1949, -0.0339, -0.0103, -0.0993, -0.0009, -0.1743, -0.0412, -0.0498, -0.3671, -2.5877, -0.3046, -4.025, -0.0624, -0.3883, -2.9867, -0.0488, -0.1714, -0.401, -1.5348, -0.5987, -2.5794, -0.0, -0.322, -0.708, -0.5559, -3.7468, -0.1745, -0.173, -0.2603, -0.9279, -0.9806, -0.2692, -0.4516, -Infinity, -36.7708, -19.7917, -10.6295, -10.209, -16.7782,