{"id": "670", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each item of inventory when it is sold. This is calculated by taking the average cost of all inventories currently available. This cost fluctuates as inventory prices change. The weighted average cost per unit is determined by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. This cost is then assigned to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. This method is often used when it's impossible to assign specific costs to individual units or when the accounting system isn't sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The recorded amount of inventory on hand using this method represents a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nCalculate the weighted average cost of inventory by adding the cost of goods purchased and dividing by the total units purchased.\nTo calculate the weighted average cost of inventory, first identify the cost of goods purchased and the total units purchased.\nIdentify the cost of goods purchased and the total units purchased, then add the cost of goods purchased and divide by the total units purchased.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 25.156, "generation_time_seconds": 22.7842, "selection_time_seconds": 2.3634, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [8304, 220, 16, 25, 70894, 279, 7286, 315, 36824, 5461, 15444, 53724, 624, 8304, 220, 17, 25, 64547, 279, 1376, 6813, 315, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 624, 8304, 220, 18, 25, 20517, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 817, 4982, 624, 8304, 220, 19, 25, 29901, 