{"id": "493", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can tell who liked your tweet:\n\nPassage 1 suggests using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm to track who has favorited your tweets. These websites show you a list of users who have favorited your tweets, allowing you to see who has shown interest in your content.\n\nPassage 2 provides a step-by-step guide on how to track who favorited your tweets. According to the passage, you can use a simple web-based website to check who favorited your tweets. Once you have access to this website, you can enter your tweet's URL to view a list of users who have favorited it.\n\nPassage 3 does not provide any information on tracking who favorited your tweets. However, it does mention that you can set up your Twitter account to notify you via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.\n\nIn summary, to tell who liked your tweet, you can use websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, or set up your Twitter account to notify you via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.", "pred": "To determine who liked your tweet, you need to identify the source of the tweet and check the engagement metrics provided by the social media platform. Analyze the specific engagement metrics such as likes, retweets, and replies to understand who interacted with the tweet. Review the user profiles of those who liked the tweet to identify the individuals who showed interest in your content.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "Answer: To determine who liked your tweet, you need to identify the source of the tweet and check the engagement metrics provided by the social media platform. Analyze the specific engagement metrics such as likes, retweets, and replies to understand who interacted with the tweet. Review the user profiles of those who liked the tweet to identify the individuals who showed interest in your content.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nIdentify the source of the tweet and check the engagement metrics provided by the social media platform.\nAnalyze the specific engagement metrics such as likes, retweets, and replies to understand who interacted with the tweet.\nReview the user profiles of those who liked the tweet to identify the individuals who showed interest in your content.\n\nAnswer: To determine who liked your tweet, you need to identify the source of the tweet and check the engagement metrics provided by the social media platform. Analyze the specific engagement metrics such as likes, retweets, and replies to understand who interacted with the tweet. Review the user profiles of those who liked the tweet to identify the individuals who showed interest in your content.", "time_sec": 24.924, "generation_time_seconds": 21.7383, "selection_time_seconds": 3.1762, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 2530, 315, 279, 11809, 323, 1779, 279, 19805, 16734, 3897, 553, 279, 3590, 3687, 5339, 13, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645], "logp_list": [-2.4368, -0.0044, -0.119, -2.8136, -0.0042, -0.0703, -1.1549, -2.1305, -1.412, -0.9643, -0.5932, -0.0159, -0.5432, -0.0035, -0.0049, -0.9241, -0.0008, -0.0, -0.1618, -0.016, -25.0, -25.4167, -5.3859, -2.97, -8.361, -8.2426, -7.0186, -7.1805, -8.5521, -6.7618, -5.7181, -5.0503, -4.6829, -3.2607, -3.384], "cumulative_logprob": -140.3586, "norm_logp": -4.0102, "input_len": 141, "output_tokens": 35, "avg_logp": -4.010249137878418}, {"beam": 0, "rollout": 1, "token_ids": [5338, 11, 358, 1184, 311, 23643, 279, 2661, 3405, 25, 330, 5158, 646, 498, 3291, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 3263, 576, 3383, 374, 311, 8253, 1246, 311, 10542, 7775, 879, 614, 14915, 264, 11809, 382], "logp_list":