{"id": "644", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "To plant a potato that has sprouted, first cut large potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, being careful not to break the long sprouts. Place these chunks on a tray and let them dry out in a cool, dark place overnight. Small, sprouted potatoes can be planted whole. Next, prepare your planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches, removing any large rocks or debris. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. Cut your potatoes into cubes of about 1 to 2 inches, making sure to leave a sprout on each cube. Place these cubes in the trench, sprouted side facing up, spaced about 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover the potatoes with compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings. Water the potatoes thoroughly and regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Finally, add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it at least 4 inches away from the sprouting potato vines. This mulch will help keep the soil cool and maintain a consistent moisture level.", "pred": "To plant a potato that has sprouted, follow these steps:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "Answer: To plant a potato that has sprouted, follow these steps:\n1. Determine the ideal time for planting, which is typically in late winter or early spring, depending on your climate.\n2. Prepare the planting site by ensuring it is well-drained and receives plenty of sunlight.\n3. Select a suitable spot in the garden or container where the potato will have space to grow, ensuring it is not shaded by other plants.\n4. Dig a hole or trench about 4-6 inches deep, depending on the variety of potato you are planting.\n5. Place the sprouted potato in the hole or trench with the sprout facing upwards, ensuring it is positioned so it will not be covered by soil.\n6. Cover the potato with soil, leaving only the sprout exposed. The sprout should be at the soil surface or slightly above it.\n7. Water the potato thoroughly after planting to help it establish roots and begin growing.\n8. Continue to monitor the potato's growth and provide additional watering and fertilization as needed. Keep the soil consistently moist but not waterlogged to promote healthy growth.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nThe reasoning steps are:\nStep 1: Determine the ideal time for planting.\nStep 2: Prepare the planting site by ensuring it is well-drained and receives plenty of sunlight.\nStep 3: Select a suitable spot in the garden or container where the potato will have space to grow.\nStep 4: Dig a hole or trench about 4-6 inches deep, depending on the variety of potato you are planting.\nStep 5: Place the sprouted potato in the hole or trench with the sprout facing upwards.\nStep 6: Cover the potato with soil, leaving only the sprout exposed.\nStep 7: Water the potato thoroughly after\nStep 8: Continue to monitor the potato's growth and provide additional watering and fertilization as needed.\n\nAnswer: To plant a potato that has sprouted, follow these steps:\n1. Determine the ideal time for planting, which is typically in late winter or early spring, depending on your climate.\n2. Prepare the planting site by ensuring it is well-drained and receives plenty of sunlight.\n3. Select a suitable spot in the garden or container where the potato will have space to grow, ensuring it is not shaded by other plants.\n4. Dig a hole or trench about 4-6 inches deep, depending on the variety of potato you are planting.\n5. Place the sprouted potato in the hole or trench with the sprout facing upwards, ensuring it is positioned so it will not be covered by soil.\n6. Cover the potato with soil, leaving only the sprout exposed. The sprout should be at the soil surface or slightly above it.\n7. Water the potato thoroughly after planting to help it establish roots and begin growing.\n8. Continue to monitor the potato's growth and provide additional watering and fertilization as needed. Keep the soil consistently moist but not waterlogged to promote healthy growth.", "time_sec": 48.245, "generation_time_seconds": 44.6562, "selection_time_seconds": 3.58, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [840, 19991, 279, 8151, 18533, 38734, 369, 11929, 315, 9314, 6513, 11, 22573, 429, 279, 8151, 11672, 525, 3746, 323, 537, 448, 12336, 13, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645], "logp_list": [-2.062, -0.0352, -0.0013, -0.3789, -0.1015, -0.0051, -0.7972, -0.7163, -0.0, -0.8745, -0.015, -0.2185, -5.8517, -0.4479, -0.0883, -0.0124, -0.3037, -0.0003, -0.823, -0.0024, -0.2701, -2.9856, -0.0102, -0.0358, -0.072, -23.5417, -23.75, -10.0001, -18.125, -9.9705, -9.5481, -11.0847, -10.2275, -9.3879, -8.6851, -8.2157, -8.5771, -7.7278, -6.6698, -6.5401, -6.1254, -6.514, -6.3334, -5.1523, -4.454, -4.8305, -6.2839, -7.1093, -8.5585, -8.547, -7.727, -5.9067, -3.5, -3.5467, -4.5378, -4.5408, -3.5378, -3.4655, -4.5587, -4.6635, -5.4854, -5.3038, -5.0825, -4.0887, -3.9553, -2.982, -3.3179, -4.228, -4.006, -5.0772, -5.1438, -4.7302, -3.9827, -4.228, -4.0116, -4.2581, -4.4165, -3.687, -2.7489, -2.4783, -1.9961, -3.5206, -3.7806, -2.7799, -3.5763, -3.579, -4.9237, -4.8573, -5.0669], "cumulative_logprob": -415.3451, "norm_logp": -4.6668, "input_len": 142, "output_tokens": 89, "avg_logp": -4.666794776916504}, {"beam": 0, "rollout": 1, "token_ids": [785, 3405, 17064, 1246,