{"id": "664", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "When you breathe in, or inhale, the intercostal muscles contract and expand the ribcage while the diaphragm contracts and pulls downward to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest and allows air to be sucked into the lungs through the windpipe. When you breathe out, or exhale, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax. This reduces the space in the chest cavity and forces out air that is rich in carbon dioxide out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "pred": "When you breathe in, your diaphragm contracts, pulling the bottom area of your lungs down, and your intercostal muscles between your ribs contract, making your rib cage expand. This combination of actions creates more space in your chest cavity, lowering the pressure inside your lungs. As a result, the air outside, which has a higher pressure, flows into your lungs to equalize the pressure.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "Answer: When you breathe in, your diaphragm contracts, pulling the bottom area of your lungs down, and your intercostal muscles between your ribs contract, making your rib cage expand. This combination of actions creates more space in your chest cavity, lowering the pressure inside your lungs. As a result, the air outside, which has a higher pressure, flows into your lungs to equalize the pressure.\n\nWhen you breathe out, your diaphragm and intercostal muscles relax, allowing the diaphragm to move up and the rib cage to contract. This reduces the volume of your chest cavity, increasing the air pressure inside your lungs. The air, now under higher pressure, is pushed out of your lungs to equalize the pressure with the lower pressure outside.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nUnderstand the physiological process of inhalation and exhalation, including the role of the diaphragm, intercostal muscles, and the movement of air into and out of the lungs.\nAnalyze the role of the diaphragm and intercostal muscles in the process of inhalation and exhalation, focusing on how they contract and expand to move air into and out of the lungs.\nAnalyze the physiological changes in the air pressure within the lungs during inhalation and exhalation, and how these changes facilitate the movement of air.\n\nAnswer: When you breathe in, your diaphragm contracts, pulling the bottom area of your lungs down, and your intercostal muscles between your ribs contract, making your rib cage expand. This combination of actions creates more space in your chest cavity, lowering the pressure inside your lungs. As a result, the air outside, which has a higher pressure, flows into your lungs to equalize the pressure.\n\nWhen you breathe out, your diaphragm and intercostal muscles relax, allowing the diaphragm to move up and the rib cage to contract. This reduces the volume of your chest cavity, increasing the air pressure inside your lungs. The air, now under higher pressure, is pushed out of your lungs to equalize the pressure with the lower pressure outside.", "time_sec": 38.79, "generation_time_seconds": 35.8555, "selection_time_seconds": 2.9256, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 2621, 279, 3405, 330, 12555, 8573, 979, 498, 11486, 304, 323, 700, 1335, 1077, 594, 1438, 432, 1495, 3019, 553, 3019, 1667, 279, 1995, 3897, 304, 279, 21085, 1447, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645], "logp_list": [-2.5958, -3.2978, -0.0139, -0.0332, -0.0836, -0.1427, 0.0, 0.0, -0.0, -1.1943, 0.0, -0.0, 0.0, -0.1233, -0.3971, -0.0011, -0.2072, -2.7756, -0.0, -0.2635, -0.043, 0.0, -1.