{"id": "199", "question": "what causes necrotizing fasciitis", "gt": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused primarily by bacteria, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci, also known as Streptococcus pyogenes. This is the same bacteria that causes strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria types, such as staphylococcus, are also associated with the disease. The bacteria infect the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue below the skin. Prior trauma, such as a cut or scrape, insect bite, burn, or needle puncture wound can serve as a source of infection as well as surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures. In rare cases, fungi can lead to this condition.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nBased on the given passage, it does not provide specific information about the causes of necrotizing fasciitis. Therefore, the next logical step would be to search for additional information or use external knowledge to determine the causes of necrotizing fasciitis.\nThe passage does not contain sufficient information to answer the question about the causes of necrotizing fasciitis.\nThe passages do not contain sufficient information to answer the question about the causes of necrotizing fasciitis.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 23.019, "generation_time_seconds": 20.3503, "selection_time_seconds": 2.6609, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 3151, 4494, 315, 23157, 429, 646, 5240, 19053, 4640, 4849, 15195, 72, 19435, 13, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 1