{"id": "294", "question": "what is the proper way to fertilize your grass", "gt": "To properly fertilize your grass, you should fertilize at the beginning of the growing season for your specific grass type. For warm season grasses, this would be when the grass turns green in the early spring. You should also know the type of fertilizer to use based on the type of grass you have and do a pH soil test to ensure the fertilizer has the appropriate pH for your soil. Once you've determined the correct fertilizer and timing, apply it in a perpendicular pattern and check the lawn after application by stepping on the turf. If the leaf blade bounces back up, the lawn is in good shape, but if it lays flat, you've over-fertilized.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nIdentify the type of grass and its specific fertilization needs. Different grass types have different optimal fertilization schedules and amounts. For example, cool-season grasses generally need more nitrogen in spring and fall, while warm-season grasses need more nitrogen in the summer.\nResearch the local climate and soil type to determine the best fertilizer type and application method. Factors such as pH level, nutrient deficiencies, and local rainfall can influence the choice of fertilizer and its application.\nConsider the specific symptoms of nutrient deficiencies to determine the type of fertilizer needed. For instance, yellowing grass may indicate a lack of iron, while thin, patchy grass might suggest a deficiency in nitrogen or phosphorus.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 25.303, "generation_time_seconds": 22.6691, "selection_time_seconds": 2.6243, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [785, 21085, 3897, 1558, 537