{"id": "675", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a brief answer to your question:\n\nThe weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. The cost of goods available for sale is divided by the number of units available for sale to calculate the weighted-average cost per unit. This figure is then used to assign a cost to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. The weighted average method is commonly used when inventory items are intermingled and cannot be assigned a specific cost, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "pred": "The weighted average inventory costing method is a method that calculates the average cost of all inventory items available for sale during the accounting period. It is used to determine the cost of goods sold (COGS) and the value of ending inventory. The method involves determining the total cost of inventory items available for sale, the total number of units available for sale, and dividing the total cost of inventory items by the total number of units available for sale. The weighted average cost per unit is then used to calculate the COGS and the ending inventory value. The weighted average method results in a more stable COGS and a more consistent ending inventory value compared to other methods.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "The weighted average inventory costing method calculates the average cost of all inventory items available for sale during the accounting period. This method is used to determine the cost of goods sold (COGS) and the value of ending inventory.\n\nTo calculate the weighted average cost:\n\n1. Determine the total cost of all inventory items available for sale during the accounting period.\n2. Determine the total number of units available for sale.\n3. Divide the total cost of inventory items by the total number of units available for sale.\n\nThis calculation results in the weighted average cost per unit. To determine the cost of goods sold, multiply the number of units sold by the weighted average cost per unit. To determine the ending inventory value, multiply the number of units remaining in inventory by the weighted average cost per unit.\n\nThe weighted average inventory costing method has the following impacts on financial statements:\n\n1. COGS: The weighted average inventory method results in a more stable COGS compared to other methods, as inventory costs are averaged over time.\n2. Ending Inventory: The ending inventory is valued at the weighted average cost per unit, providing a more consistent value compared to other methods.\n\nAnswer: The weighted average inventory costing method is a method that calculates the average cost of all inventory items available for sale during the accounting period. It is used to determine the cost of goods sold (COGS) and the value of ending inventory. The method involves determining the total cost of inventory items available for sale, the total number of units available for sale, and dividing the total cost of inventory items by the total number of units available for sale. The weighted average cost per unit is then used to calculate the COGS and the ending inventory value. The weighted average method results in a more stable COGS and a more consistent ending inventory value compared to other methods.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nIdentify the key components of the weighted average inventory costing method.\nExplain the calculation process of the weighted average inventory costing method, including how costs are averaged over different inventory items.\nAnalyze the impact of using the weighted average inventory costing method on cost of goods sold (COGS) and ending inventory values.\n\nThe weighted average inventory costing method calculates the average cost of all inventory items available for sale during the accounting period. This method is used to determine the cost of goods sold (COGS) and the value of ending inventory.\n\nTo calculate the weighted average cost:\n\n1. Determine the total cost of all inventory items available for sale during the accounting period.\n2. Determine the total number of units available for sale.\n3. Divide the total cost of inventory items by the total number of units available for sale.\n\nThis calculation results in the weighted average cost per unit. To determine the cost of goods sold, multiply the number of units sold by the weighted average cost per unit. To determine the ending inventory value, multiply the number of units remaining in inventory by the weighted average cost per unit.\n\nThe weighted average inventory costing method has the following impacts on financial statements:\n\n1. COGS: The weighted average inventory method results in a more stable COGS compared to other methods, as inventory costs are averaged over time.\n2. Ending Inventory: The ending inventory is valued at the weighted average cost per unit, providing a more consistent value compared to other methods.\n\nAnswer: The weighted average inventory costing method is a method that calculates the average cost of all inventory items available for sale during the accounting period. It is used to determine the cost of goods sold (COGS) and the value of ending inventory. The method involves determining the total cost of inventory items available for sale, the total number of units available for sale, and dividing the total cost of inventory items by the total number of units available for sale. The weighted average cost per unit is then used to calculate the COGS and the ending inventory value. The weighted average method results in a more stable COGS and a more consistent ending inventory value compared to other methods.", "time_sec": 44.044, "generation_time_seconds": 40.4676, 