{"id": "516", "question": "history of the word gemini", "gt": "Sure! Here's a brief history of the word Gemini based on the given passages:\n\nThe word \"Gemini\" comes from Latin, meaning \"twins.\" The constellation Gemini was first mentioned in Aristotle's Meteorologica over 300 years BC. In Greek mythology, the twins represented in Gemini are Castor and Pollux, who share the same mother, Leda, but have different fathers. The constellation Gemini is not in the same position as the constellation of Gemini and is a mutable sign that announces change and rules the time of year when Taurus spring has ended.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you need further clarification or if there's anything else I can help you with.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nNext logical reasoning step: Identify the origin of the word \"Gemini\" and explain how it evolved over time.\nNext logical reasoning step: Describe the earliest known usage of the word \"Gemini\" and any cultural or historical contexts in which it was used.\nNext logical reasoning step: Analyze how the concept of \"Gemini\" has been represented or referenced in different historical periods and cultures, and discuss any notable shifts or developments in its meaning or usage.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 22.738, "generation_time_seconds": 19.7965, "selection_time_seconds": 2.9341, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 6028, 8544, 25, 576, 3405, 17064, 911, 279, 3840, 315, 279, 3409, 330, 95040, 6591, 1189, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 15164