{"id": "489", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "gt": "You can see who liked your tweets by using tracking websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm. These sites can give you an idea of who is favouriting your tweets. Alternatively, you can also set up notifications on Twitter itself. By going to your account settings and clicking on the notifications link, you can arrange to be notified via email whenever someone likes one of your tweets.", "pred": "To determine who liked your tweet, you would typically need to access the analytics or engagement metrics provided by the platform where you posted the tweet. These metrics usually include the number of likes, retweets, and comments. The platform may also provide a list of usernames of those who liked your tweet, allowing you to identify individual users who showed interest in your content.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "Answer: To determine who liked your tweet, you would typically need to access the analytics or engagement metrics provided by the platform where you posted the tweet. These metrics usually include the number of likes, retweets, and comments. The platform may also provide a list of usernames of those who liked your tweet, allowing you to identify individual users who showed interest in your content.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nTo determine who liked your tweet, you would typically need to access the analytics or engagement metrics provided by the platform where you posted the tweet. These metrics usually include the number of likes, retweets, and comments. The platform may also provide a list of usernames of those who liked your tweet, allowing you to identify individual users who showed interest in your content.\nThe reasoning steps are:\nTo access the analytics or engagement metrics for your tweet, you would usually log into your account on the social media platform where you posted the tweet. Once logged in, navigate to the section of the platform that displays analytics or engagement metrics. This section typically offers detailed information about the performance of your tweet, including the number of likes, retweets, and comments. In some platforms, there may be an option to view the usernames of those who liked your tweet.\n\nAnswer: To determine who liked your tweet, you would typically need to access the analytics or engagement metrics provided by the platform where you posted the tweet. These metrics usually include the number of likes, retweets, and comments. The platform may also provide a list of usernames of those who liked your tweet, allowing you to identify individual users who showed interest in your content.", "time_sec": 37.403, "generation_time_seconds": 34.6595, "selection_time_seconds": 2.7321, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [785, 1482, 1995, 1558, 537, 3410, 3565, 389, 1246, 311, 8253, 421, 4325, 14915, 264, 11809, 13, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.0826, -4.3417, -0.6829, -1.1247, -0.0, -0.0682, -1.7334, -0.1018, -0.007, -0.0046, -0.0633, -2.9703, -0.0072, -0.0007, -0.1731, -0.014, -0.0243, -0.1463, -31.4583, -25.0, -13.5161, -21.2501, -17.5002, -13.1333, -12.2954, -12.2937, -11.8855, -11.6863, -12.0997, -10.0823, -9.5015, -8.8361, -7.6984, -5.7661, -5.3658, -6.3657, -6.5224, -6.5951, -7.1025, -7.9076, -8.9518, -9.1242, -9.3557, -8.4112, -8.4373, -7.9577, -8.8815, -8.39, -7.4396, -6.3083, -7.2431, -8.8365, -9.6739, -9.3557, -8.7564, -