{"id": "647", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to plant a potato that has sprouted:\n\n1. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, making sure to not break the long sprouts.\n2. Allow the cut ends to dry out overnight in a cool, dark location.\n3. Plant the potato chunks or whole potatoes in well-draining soil, spacing them 24 to 36 inches apart.\n4. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, and place the potato pieces in the bottom of the trench with the sprouted side facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\n\nNote that the passages do not provide specific information on how to care for the potatoes after planting, such as watering and fertilization.", "pred": "To plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_gd", "raw_output": "Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\n1. Choose a well-draining soil that is rich in organic matter. Avoid heavy clay soils as they can retain too much water and cause the potato to rot.\n2. Prepare the soil by turning it over with a spade or tiller to aerate it and incorporate any organic matter, such as compost or well-rotted manure.\n3. Space the sprouted potatoes in rows, with the sprouts facing upwards. Dig a furrow that is about 4-6 inches deep along the row.\n4. Place the sprouted potatoes in the furrow, with the sprouts at the soil surface and the eyes (buds) facing upwards. Space the potatoes about 12-18 inches apart.\n5. Cover the sprouted potatoes with soil, leaving only the sprouts exposed. This will allow the potatoes to grow and develop a good root system.\n6. Water the potatoes regularly, but do not overwater, as they can rot in wet soil. Keep the soil consistently moist, but allow the soil to dry out between watering sessions.\n7. Continue to monitor the potatoes for signs of sprouting and regrowth, and harvest them when they are ready.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nIdentify the appropriate planting method for sprouted potatoes.\nConsider the soil conditions required for planting a sprouted potato.\nExamine the size and health of the sprouted potato before planting.\n\nAnswer: To plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\n1. Choose a well-draining soil that is rich in organic matter. Avoid heavy clay soils as they can retain too much water and cause the potato to rot.\n2. Prepare the soil by turning it over with a spade or tiller to aerate it and incorporate any organic matter, such as compost or well-rotted manure.\n3. Space the sprouted potatoes in rows, with the sprouts facing upwards. Dig a furrow that is about 4-6 inches deep along the row.\n4. Place the sprouted potatoes in the furrow, with the sprouts at the soil surface and the eyes (buds) facing upwards. Space the potatoes about 12-18 inches apart.\n5. Cover the sprouted potatoes with soil, leaving only the sprouts exposed. This will allow the potatoes to grow and develop a good root system.\n6. Water the potatoes regularly, but do not overwater, as they can rot in wet soil. Keep the soil consistently moist, but allow the soil to dry out between watering sessions.\n7. Continue to monitor the potatoes for signs of sprouting and regrowth, and harvest them when they are ready.", "time_sec": 38.938, "generation_time_seconds": 35.501, "selection_time_seconds": 3.4282, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 943, 315, 38734, 8045, 429, 702, 8151, 18533, 323, 2908, 1181, 3151, 7826, 4682, 13, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645], "logp_list": [-2.062, -0.0008, -0.0045, -3.2711, -0.0102, -0.0879, -6.331, -1.9334, -0.0444, -0.0012, -0.0, -2.1228, -0.73